# Feeds: branches, threads and conversations

Status: Rich agreed the requirements on 2026-10-25. This document also gives a review of the trajectory
design (dxos/dxos#13782) and a counter-proposal. The counter-proposal is for discussion with Dima. The
code implements forks, the first phase [D-2]. Merge, close, heads and partial sync are not in the code.

Rich and Dima reviewed the design on 2026-10-26. The decisions of
that review are in the changelog ([D-2] to [D-20]).

This document combines the previous design documents for feeds. The source documents are in
[`feeds/`](./feeds), beside this document. Each source document gives all of its reasons:

| Source                                                       | What it decided                                                                         | State                         |
| ------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| [soft-fork.md](./feeds/soft-fork.md)                         | Rewind in one feed, with a lineage for each item in `@meta` (`Feed.PARENT_KEY`).        | Done (#12387)                 |
| [live-objects.md](./feeds/live-objects.md)                   | Feed items are live objects. An update appends the full object again. A scale roadmap.  | Done (#12235). Roadmap        |
| [agent-feed-messages.md](./feeds/agent-feed-messages.md)     | The queue and the alarms of an agent are in its feed as annotated records.              | Done. #13782 replaces it      |
| [private-threads.md](./feeds/private-threads.md)             | Four designs for hidden agent threads. It recommends a child feed for each thread.      | Analysis only                 |
| [thread-channel-merge.md](./feeds/thread-channel-merge.md)   | `Thread` and `Channel` are one concept. It recommends one conversation type.            | Analysis only                 |
| `plugin-review/docs/COMMENT-FEEDS.md`                        | Comment threads on a feed that the thread owns. The assistant replies in a hidden chat. | Stage 1 in #14206             |
| `compute/trajectory` README and `LIGHT-THREADS.mdl` (#13782) | Chat feeds become a log of `Trajectory.Event`s. Threads are ids on events.              | Open PR                       |

## Extensions

This document uses these extension dialects.

| Term       | URI                         |
| ---------- | --------------------------- |
| `feat`     | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`     |
| `type`     | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`     |
| `module`   | `org.dxos.mdl.module@1.0`   |
| `decision` | `org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0` |

## Principle

Comments, AI chats and message threads are the same thing. Each is an ordered branch of messages from
several participants. Some participants can be agents. The difference is the container object that points
at the branch:

| Container      | Adds                                    |
| -------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| Comment thread | an anchor on a subject, and a status    |
| Chat           | a model, instructions, and tasks        |
| Channel        | an external backend (Bluesky, email, …) |

Thus storage needs one model, and the containers stay thin. This document has the same conclusion as
[thread-channel-merge.md](./feeds/thread-channel-merge.md), one level down. That document merges the
container types. This document gives the containers a common store.

## Requirements

```mdl
feat F-1: Model

  req F-1.1: Feed. A feed is one logical append log of ECHO objects. Usually these objects are
    `Message` objects. A feed also holds the records that its users need (bindings, turn markers,
    alarms).

  req F-1.2: Branch tree. A feed is a tree of branches. Each branch has a parent branch and a fixed
    fork point. A read of a branch gives the items of its ancestors up to the fork point, and then the
    items of the branch. Branches are lightweight: they need **no additional objects in the ECHO
    graph**. The `Feed` object can hold a map or an index of its branches, but the feed is
    self-contained.

  req F-1.3: Rewrite. You can upsert an item by id, atomically. A reader sees the old version or the
    new version. A reader never sees the two versions, and a reader never sees no version. Use an
    upsert to edit a message, for a reply that replaces its pending line, and to stream partial output.
    The feed accepts an update of an object only on the branch of the object. A change on a different
    branch is a fork, and it must merge back into the main branch [D-12].

  req F-1.4: Logical removal. You can remove a branch from reads, indexes and sync. Its items stay in
    the log until retention removes them.

  req F-1.5: Rewind. A resume from an earlier item creates a branch at that point and removes the old
    branch [F-1.4]. Rewind is not a separate mechanism.

  req F-1.6: Owner. A branch has an owner [D-8].

  req F-1.7: Agent branches. An agent creates a new branch when the owner of the base branch is a
    different device [D-9].
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Read, index and sync

  req F-2.1: Index all or main. Indexing is a separate layer, and a feed can have no index. The index
    of a feed holds all branches or only the main branch. It does not hold a selected subset of
    branches. The default is the main branch only [D-10]. A feed of messages from people indexes all
    branches. A feed whose items mutate objects indexes only the main branch [D-11]. A query can have a
    scope of a set of branches and a time range. The query gives the newest items first, and it applies
    the limit after the branch filter.

  req F-2.2: Changed heads. Each branch has a head. One cheap call gives the heads that changed since
    cursor C. The call does not scan items. Triggers use this call.

  req F-2.3: Partial sync. A client syncs all branches or a subset of branches. A client can sync one
    branch with its ancestors up to the fork point, without the remaining branches of the tree.

  req F-2.4: Two orders. Readers can use the two orders. The EDGE position converges across peers.
    The local order never changes under a reader, thus a cached fold stays valid.
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Uses

  req F-3.1: AI chat, comments and messaging all use this model.

  req F-3.2: Feed kinds. There are three kinds of feed: feeds that mutate objects (upserts), feeds of
    messages from people, and feeds of AI history (trajectories). One feed does not mix these kinds
    [D-13].

  req F-3.3: A feed for each object. At the application level, an object (a document, a spreadsheet, a
    canvas) has a ref to a feed, as a chat has a ref to a feed. The comments plugin operates on the feed
    of the object. The target is one feed for the comments of an object and one feed for its agent
    operations. ECHO does not give each object a feed yet [D-14]. A feed for each object is independent
    of the branches in a feed [D-16].

  req F-3.4: One logical session. An object has one logical session, and the session has branches. The
    application can create separate sessions, but it does not have to [D-15].
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Cross-cutting

  req F-4.1: Hidden by construction. A default read covers one branch. Thus a private branch never
    gets to a reader that did not ask for it.

  req F-4.2: Several writers. The system never misattributes concurrent appends. The view of a turn
    is fixed when the turn starts. A reader can detect an item that arrives late.

  req F-4.3: Cost. A subject can have many branches, and there can be many subjects. A new branch
    costs approximately one record.

  req F-4.4: Retention. Each branch can have its own trim policy. A trim of a branch never trims its
    ancestors.

  req F-4.5: Permissions. The design does not prevent a branch that has a smaller audience than its
    parent. No code does this yet.
```

Merge and close. Forks come first. Merge and close are a follow-up [D-2]. These points are agreed:

- Close and merge are two different operations. A branch can close without a merge. Then its parent does
  not know that the branch closed [D-3].
- A merge tells the parent branch that the branch was finalized into it. The core does nothing with a
  merge. A reader sees the merge message when it iterates the parent [D-3].
- A merge folds in one branch at a time, and the merge sits on the main line [D-4].

Open: does a reader of the main line see branch messages and merge messages, but not always close
messages? Rich thinks so, and he will write this part out.

## What exists

### Feed and blocks

A `Feed` is an ECHO object. The feed store (`FeedStore`, `org.dxos.feed`) keeps the items of a feed as
blocks.

```mdl
type Block
  desc: |
    A feed item in the feed store. A block holds `actorId`, `sequence`, `prevActorId`,
    `prevSequence`, a timestamp and the encrypted payload.
  fields:
    position: only EDGE sets it, from one counter for each space and namespace. Thus you can compare positions across the feeds of a space.
    insertionId: local to the device. #13782 shows it as local order (`Filter.feedCursor({ order: 'local' })`, `Feed.getLocalCursor`).
```

- **Upsert.** `Obj.update` on a feed item appends the full object again under its id. The index keeps
  the latest block for each id. For concurrent writers, the last flush wins for each object. (Refer to
  [live-objects.md](./feeds/live-objects.md). Stage 3 of its roadmap adds versions for writes.)
- **Delete.** `Feed.remove` appends a tombstone. The index merges the tombstone onto the previous
  snapshot.
- **Options.** `Feed.indexing` (on or off, with no partial mode) and `Feed.retention` (`maxLength`,
  `maxAge`) apply to one full feed (#14199, `org.dxos.echo` [F-7]).
- **Sync.** A client pulls the full feed. (Refer to live-objects.md. Stage 2 of its roadmap is a
  bounded tail.)

### Soft fork (a lineage for each item)

An item can name the item that it continues from (`Feed.PARENT_KEY` in `@meta`). `Feed.history` starts
at the head, goes back, and removes the items that a fork abandoned. `Feed.rewindFrom` holds a pending
rewind. The design of soft fork ([soft-fork.md](./feeds/soft-fork.md)) rejected three alternatives. These
alternatives are important for this document:

- **Lineage on the block**: two writers already fork the block chain. Thus a block parent cannot tell
  the difference between a deliberate fork and a race. Also, this alternative is a change to the wire,
  SQL and EDGE.
- **Sidecar fork markers** (a `Fork` item that captures all items after it): the system misattributes a
  writer that did not see the marker to the branch.
- **Branch state on the `Feed` object**: the object (Automerge) and the blocks replicate on different
  paths. Thus a fork can become visible before or after the items that it describes.

The design deferred three items: a list of branches, a stored head for each branch, and a branch filter
in queries. (A `limit` truncates before `history` resolves.)

### Hard fork (`SessionLink`)

`ForkChat` creates a new feed. The first record of the new feed points at a message in the source feed.
`SessionStore.reifyHistory` prepends the source up to that message. The cutoff is fixed, the order is by
`created`, and only the first link counts.

### Private threads (analysis)

[private-threads.md](./feeds/private-threads.md) compared four designs:

- tags on items in one feed (A)
- a child feed for each thread (B)
- a hidden chat for each thread (C)
- real branches in one feed (D).

It recommended B for two reasons. A leaks by default. D needs an indexed branch column, and the index
does not have this column. Annotations are post-filters, thus a filter of a feed by tag scans the full
feed.

### Comments (stage 1, #14206)

`Thread` 0.3.0 owns a feed of `Message`s. The comment agent runs a hidden `Chat` on the same feed. The
comment UI hides the synthetic prompts and the tool traffic of the agent with a filter on visible blocks.
This is a small example of the failure of design A.

### Trajectory (#13782)

Chat feeds become an append-only log of immutable `Trajectory.Event`s:

```mdl
type Trajectory.Event
  desc: |
    An immutable event of a chat feed. Each event has `thread`, `prev`, `sender`, `created` and a
    tagged `payload`.
  fields:
    thread?: the id of the `threadOpen` or `turnBegin` that opened its thread. It is not set for main.
    prev: the head of that thread for the writer.
```

```mdl
type threadOpen
  desc: |
    Opens a thread. `threadMerge`, `threadClose` and `threadWait` end, close or join a thread. A turn
    is also a thread. `fork` continues a different feed up to a fixed event. `rewind` discards main
    from an event.
  fields:
    mode: fork | fresh | light
    from: { feed?, thread?, event }
```

- Reducers fold the feed in local order into the views that the UI, the agent and the prompt need.
  SQLite keeps checkpoints of the folded state.
- New chat feeds have indexing off.

## Review of the trajectory design

The table compares the trajectory design with the requirements:

| Req     | Trajectory                                                                                    | Verdict |
| ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |
| [F-1.1] | Items are `Trajectory.Event`s with the content as a payload, not arbitrary ECHO objects.      | Gap     |
| [F-1.2] | Threads are ids on events. No extra objects. Nested. A fixed origin (`from.event`).           | Meets   |
| [F-1.3] | Events are immutable. An edit or a replaced pending line is a new event for the reducer.      | Partial |
| [F-1.4] | `threadClose { status: cancelled }`.                                                          | Meets   |
| [F-1.5] | `rewind` discards main from an event. You cannot rewind a thread.                             | Partial |
| [F-2.1] | Chat feeds have no index. The thread id is in the payload, and no index can filter by it.     | Gap     |
| [F-2.2] | A checkpoint head for each trajectory and reducer, not for each thread.                       | Gap     |
| [F-2.3] | The feed is the sync unit. You cannot sync one thread alone.                                  | Gap     |
| [F-2.4] | Local order for folds. The EDGE position is also available.                                   | Meets   |
| [F-3.1] | Agent chat only. Comments and channels hold `Message`s.                                       | Gap     |
| [F-4.1] | `TrajectoryStore` is the only reader, and it filters by thread. A fold sees all threads.      | Partial |
| [F-4.2] | `prev`, `from.event`, `PromptState.stale`, the first consume wins.                            | Meets   |
| [F-4.3] | One event for each thread opener.                                                             | Meets   |
| [F-4.4] | Retention applies to one full feed.                                                           | Gap     |
| [F-4.5] | For each feed.                                                                                | Gap     |

The concurrency model [F-4.2] and the cost model [F-1.2], [F-4.3] are the strongest parts. We recommend
that the design keeps them.

All the gaps have one cause: **the branch logic is in `@dxos/trajectory`, in the
event payload. Thus ECHO cannot see it.** The index cannot filter by thread. Sync cannot select a thread.
Retention cannot target a thread. Only agent chats can use it.

## Proposal: branches in the feed layer

Move the thread fields of the trajectory out of the payload, into the feed layer. Then all feeds have
these fields, and each ECHO object can be in a branch. Trajectory keeps its event vocabulary and its
reducers. ECHO gives it indexing, partial sync and a policy for each branch.

1. **Branch membership on the item.** Two keys in item `@meta`, next to `PARENT_KEY`:
   - `org.dxos.key.feed-branch`: the branch id. The branch id is the id of the first item of the
     branch. If the key is absent, the item is in the root branch.
   - `org.dxos.key.feed-fork`, only on the first item of a branch: the id of the fork item. The parent
     branch is the branch of the fork item [D-22].

   The branch id is the id of the base block of the branch [D-6], which is the first item of the branch
   [D-21]. The fork point is a flag [D-5]. `Feed.readBranch` gives the view of one branch: the items of
   its ancestors up to each fork point, then its own items [D-23].

   Each item states its membership, as with the lineage of soft fork. Thus the system never
   misattributes a writer that did not see a fork. The truth replicates with the blocks that it
   describes. There are no new ECHO objects.

2. **Branch policy on the `Feed` object.** `Feed.branches` (below) extends the feed options of #14199.
   It holds only policy. It never holds membership or heads. Thus the order problem of soft-fork.md does
   not apply. A policy can arrive before or after an item. The policy changes how the system treats the
   item. It does not change the branch of the item.

3. **Index by branch.** The indexer stores the branch id in a column on feed items (an `echo-sqlite`
   migration). The indexer obeys the index mode of the feed: all branches or the main branch [F-2.1],
   [D-17]. A read of a branch that the index does not hold comes from the feed store [D-25]. A scope with
   no branch filter reads only the main branch, and `'all'` reads all branches [D-24]. `Scope.feed` (below) pushes the
   branch filter and the time filter into SQL, thus `limit` applies after them. Newest first is an
   `orderBy`.

4. **Heads.** The index keeps the head of each `(feed, branch)` as a position and a local cursor.
   `Feed.heads` (below) gives the branches whose heads moved. Triggers use it. Reducers use it to decide
   what to fold again.

5. **Partial sync.** EDGE must select blocks by branch, and it must not decrypt them. Thus the branch id
   is on the block and also in `@meta`. This is the only wire change, and it is the main cost of the
   proposal. Until this change is in the code, sync stays per feed, and requirement [F-2.3] is not met.

6. **Rewind is a branch.** A rewind to item X opens a branch, with a fork at the predecessor of X. It
   also sets the old branch to `removed`. `PARENT_KEY` and `rewindFrom` continue to work for old feeds.
   We can remove them later.

7. **Upsert stays an append of the full object**, and it is atomic for each object. A change to many
   objects is one batched append.

8. **Containers point at a branch.** A comment thread, a chat or a channel holds `{ feed, branch }`. The
   comments of a subject are branches of one feed. The turns of the comment agent are branches of the
   branch of the comment thread. Thus the thread shows only the text that people wrote and the final
   reply of the agent.

9. **Trajectory on top.** A trajectory thread is a feed branch. `thread` and `prev` move from the event
   into `@meta`. `threadMerge`, `threadWait` and the turn semantics stay as trajectory events. Reducers
   read one branch and its ancestors. They do not filter the full feed.

10. **Cursors.** A cursor is `(branch, pos)`. A cursor can contain branch points [D-7].

```mdl
module Feed
  desc: Proposed members of the Feed module (items 2 and 4 above).
  api:
    branches: Record<BranchId, { removed?, retention?, name? }>  # only branch policy, never membership or heads.
    heads: (feed, { since }) => branches whose heads moved                   # for triggers, and for reducers that decide what to fold again.
```

```mdl
module Scope
  desc: Proposed member of the Scope module (item 3 above).
  api:
    feed: (uri, { branches, after, before })  # pushes the branch filter and the time filter into SQL, thus `limit` applies after them. No branches is main only; 'all' reads all branches.
```

## Questions for Dima

1. Did you put the thread ids in the payload deliberately, because then ECHO does not change? Or, if we
   move the thread ids to `@meta`, do the reducers lose something that they need?
2. Chat feeds have indexing off because no code read them through the index. With a branch column and
   push-down, do you want an index for chat feeds again? Or do you want no index for chat feeds, and an
   index only for comment branches and channel branches?

   Answer: the index of a feed holds all branches or only the main branch, and the default is the main
   branch [F-2.1]. Dima thinks about no index for trajectory feeds, because a reducer builds the state of
   the conversation.
3. Soft fork rejected block-level lineage. Is a block-level branch id (item 5 of the proposal) an
   acceptable wire change? The reasons are different: a lineage edge on blocks mixed races with forks,
   but a branch id does not.
4. Is merge a concept of the feed layer, or does merge stay a trajectory event?

   Answer: the core does nothing with a merge [D-3]. Merge and close are a follow-up [D-2].
5. Can a `Message` be directly in a trajectory feed? Or must an event always wrap the content? Comments
   and channels need `Message` objects that other code reads, edits and indexes.

